tate or tumble at a  much slower rate and because of that we  experienced a significant amount of line  broadening so in order to compensate for  that we typically need to acquire our  data for longer or take more scans of  our data  this was actually illustrated a few  years back in a paper produced out of  this out of our group as well that doing  or performing a domain cleavage meaning  doing a digest of the intact map into  its fa b and FC domains does not  actually alter the higher order  structure but we just require more scans  of the intact map in order to be able to  dig down deeper and and account for the  line broadening so we did additional  correlations outside of just  those four samples I showed you and as  you can see once again profile for the  most part across the spectrometers gives  you fairly comparable data with respect  to the 700 megahertz spectrometer data  you will see that there is some  discrepancy with there compared to the  other three and that is due mostly in  part to an issue that was issue with  water suppression of the of the sample  setup or the experimental setup so there  the reason that is the reason that ADA  doesn't necessarily match if we go over  to the 2d methyl fingerprinting data of  the same sample correlations we begin to  see a very significant discrepancy  between our across all of the  spectrometers with the exception of the  600 megahertz  a and 900 megahertz spectrum the reason  for that well and I'll get into that in  a minute I want to show you the data  first so sample 12-day our sample 12  which is a blended sample this is the  spectrum of the sample taken at 900  megahertz and I will point out that the  900 megahertz spectrometer data is  actually the some of the very first data  that was collected if we look at it  compared to the 600 megahertz  spectrometer data um you will see that  there are some significant discrepancies  with the data and that has to do with  the fact that the 600 megahertz spectrum  are 600 meters B data was actually  collected at the very end of the study  which was close to a year later we see  that with respect to the blended sample  sample 11 the same kind of discrepancy  and sample 10 which is also a different  percentage of the same kind of blended  sample we see once again a discrepancy  in and I'm just highlighting in these  cases just some of the peaks that that  change and then some of the deviations  we see so what we were able to conclude  about this is that there was some form  of sample degradation over time in this  case we  you see is the methyl fingerprint of the  spectra collected at 600 megahertz of  one of the samples and the blue is the  initial data that was collected which  was the very first spectroscopic data  collected on this sample or the very  first spectrometer used and then we see  in the red the data collected on the  exact same sample after year on the  exact same spectrometer and as you don't  need to be in a more spectroscopy to be  able to see that there is distinct  difference between the two samples this  tells us that there's some kind of  change occurring whether it's a change  in higher order structure or some form  of chemical modification that is  occurring so flush this out a little bit  more we collected some mass spectroscopy  data on it and compared it to what we  expect to actually see I'm so in the  first column from the left you see the  NIST the title for NIST map RM 86 71  which is what samples 10 11 and 12 are  also but at blended States and you can  see the relative amount of oxidation  occurring at each of the specific  methines associated with that as you  move across the board you can see that  each of these samples has some oxidation  occurring at different sites so as you  can see here Metheny in 34 has a  significant amount of oxidation compared  to what you would expect to find methane  101 has probably the highest level of  oxidation compared to what you'd expect  to find and then methionine 255 also has  for the most part some extensive  oxidation as well which goes to explain  why we are seeing what is occurring in  those spectra so to just illustrate some  of the outcomes that we've seen from  this study one the profile method and  the 2d fingerprinting method for the  most part provide a fairly comparable  data in these instances that we showed  with the exception of being that the  the degraded samples were less sensitive  or less able to be detected by the 1d  method or the 1d profile method I should  say than the 2d fingerprinting method  and this could be due in part to the  fact that when you add an extra  dimension of detection you can pick up  on a few more things that you maybe  won't necessarily be able to pick up on  with 1d if it's a very low level of  chemical modification now if these were  most more extensive you would probably  see that in profile quite well so from  that I want to move on to some work that  we've been doing that is very much  related to this using low resolution  techniques of NMR as a screening  technique for higher order structure one  of the issues that we run into as NMR  spectroscopy is most people think that  you need a very big super expensive high  field magnet under megahertz instrument  which is going to run in the range of  millions of dollars in order to acquire  these kinds of spectral data so we  wanted to go and see if we could  actually acquire good data and if it was  capable of giving us the information  that we wanted at low field so here we  use four different samples which I have  listed and we acquired the same kind of  diffusion experiments that you would  collect using profile so the post field  gradient stimulated echo experiments and  we did this at 600 megahertz but with a  cryoprobe but we also did this at a  lower field at 400 megahertz with a room  temperature probe and for those who  aren't don't know what I'm talking about  as far as the differences the lower and  field strengths you go obviously the  less the sensitivity but you also lose a  significant amount of sensitivity when  you use a room temperature probe instead  of a cryo probe and the reason we wanted  to look at these specific setups is many  companies have access to a 400 megahertz  NMR with this with a standard probe much  fewer or many fewer companies have  access to higher field issues such as  six seven eight and 900  it's in Amar's with very expensive cryo  platform system setup so we collected  this data and what we were able to  decipher is that using the profile  method so this is the 1d data that was  processed using the profile correlation  analysis we see a similar trend across  the board with these samples the issue  lies in the scaling of the difference of  these samples or the correlation  similarity of these samples so if you  look on the left the left column the two  spectra are the two plots you see on the  left are both at 600 megahertz with  1,000 scans this data takes  approximately an hour and 30 minutes to  acquire and and the top and bottom are  the exact same data points I just  organized that way to illustrate a point  if you look on the right column the two  box spots the top is at 256 scans using  a 400 megahertz spectrometer and the  bottom is at 512 scans using a 400  megahertz spectrometer the difference in  time is at 512 using this setup it takes  approximately 40 ish minutes to acquire  a spectrum and at 206 scans it takes  approximately 20 minutes to acquire the  spectrum so in 256 scans we can acquire  essentially five spectra to get somewhat  significant statistical values in  roughly the time it takes to acquire one  spectrum at 600 megahertz with 1000  scans now as I mentioned previously the  issue that we run into here is while the  trend is the same if you look at the  y-axis scales on these box plots you'll  notice that the 600 megahertz day it  only goes down to about 0.85  in the correlation value while the 400  megahertz data goes down to around 0.5  so we do see a similar trend but we  don't see the statistical value that's  being output being the same and that  does pose somewhat of a problem if we  are trying to go across field strings so  we are looking at a different method of  trying to analyze this data  we can get somewhat statistically more  meaningful data so can we use principle  component analysis to do this well PCA  or principal component analysis has been  used on several occasions to analyze 2d  NMR spectra specifically the methyl  fingerprinting method and what I'm  showing here is from a paper that was  recently published in our group where  they did a cross or an inter laboratory  study and were able to use PCA to  cluster the different sample types  regardless of the spectrometer that was  used or the user so the thought process  was well at low field strength with low  resolution we're probably not going to  be able to acquire 2d data but can we do  this kind of analysis for 1d data so  here we applied where we try to apply  that thought process so you see a series  of 1d spectra that were collected and  does it work well we think so and we're  still refining this but we're able to  acquire 1d data at low field this is 400  megahertz with 512 scans of each of  these samples took about 40 minutes to  acquire or each and as you can see the  clustering is very good from pca and not  only is the clustering very good but the  variation between clusters the distance  between clusters is fairly pronounced so  we can actually see a difference we're  currently in the process of developing a  method in order to determine whether or  not what we're seeing is statistically  significant so that's a still a work in  progress but at this point it's very  promising and we're hoping to be able to  push this out the door in the very near  future so just as a quick summary - I'll  review everything 1d profile and 2d  fingerprinting both provide comparable  data as long as we are using a linear  correlation algorithm we are looking at  developing PCA methods for 1d and seeing  if that gives us similar information to  what we would expect  we did see that 2d methyl fingerprinting  is a little bit more sensitive to low  levels of chemical modification then the  profile method was but we're also  testing to see if if the PCA method can  give us a similar kind of output on the  1d data we also found that low level or  low field low resolution 1d spectroscopy  methods are sufficient for screening at  least and but more robust statistical  analysis need to be implemented to make  it really something that can be useful  to the pharma industry and then the 1d  PCA method we're actually developing  into a toolkit in a more pipe and  hopefully we'll be able to put that out  the door to give other people access to  the ability and with that I would like  to thank and acknowledge people who are  involved in this study in these studies  so dr. Luke Arbogast and this the dr.  Matt's face drum at Amgen dr. John  marina at NIST dr. Frankel uglyness dr.  Wrenn salinas and doctors yves amon and  amon Gus Ronnie at Health Canada and  then doctors John Gibbs and John she'll  also hear and his to provided us with  and with that I would like to thank all  of you for attending and listening
 

